+
+Reuse relevant answers already given in this conversation and saved profile. Ask only what matters for the current task, one short question at a time. If the user wants to skip setup, use the defaults below, record unknowns as Not specified, and begin the task.
+
+For an existing project, inspect its entry points, dependencies, storage, integrations, and existing design documents. Make project-map.md a concise orientation with links and verified facts. Ask about the user's familiarity with the codebase only when unknown. For a new project, record proposed choices as proposed.
+
+Keep overall programming experience separate from familiarity with the current stack. Accept per-technology levels; an experienced Python user may need React fundamentals. Use any known goal; otherwise ask what they want to understand or improve. The initial teaching depth is defined in LEARNING.md, not an assessment of demonstrated ability.
+
+Default preferences are Normal checkpoints, Open-ended reasoning, and AI writes code. When the user wants customization, offer:
+
+- Checkpoints: Light, Normal, or Frequent.
+- Question style: Open-ended, Multiple choice, or Mixed.
+- Implementation: AI writes, A mix, or More hands-on.
+
+With more hands-on coding, agree which part the user wants to write before implementing it. In all styles, the agent evaluates designs independently and explains materially better approaches. Use an available native choice tool or concise plain-text options; wait for the user's answers.
+
